Task:
Build an Ordo orchestration seed that schedules tasks across candidate nodes using preference order, budget, capacity, ramp promotion, and backpressure vibration signals.

Required public API:
pub struct Plan { pub task_id: String, pub node: String, pub promoted: bool, pub signals: Vec<String> }
pub struct Scheduler
impl Scheduler {
    pub fn new() -> Self
    pub fn add_node(&mut self, id: &str, cost: u32, capacity: usize)
    pub fn add_ramp(&mut self, from: &str, to: &str)
    pub fn schedule(&self, task_id: &str, candidates: &[&str], max_cost: u32) -> Option<Plan>
}

CLI contract:
Commands: node NAME COST CAPACITY, ramp FROM TO, schedule TASK_ID BUDGET CANDIDATE...
schedule prints exactly: task=<id> node=<node> promoted=<bool> signals=<comma-list-or-none>

Requirements:
- Output exactly three files: Cargo.toml, src/lib.rs, src/main.rs.
- Use only the Rust standard library.
- Package name must be rust_ordo_suite.
- No explanations outside file blocks.
- Keep the Ordo/spiderweb shape: flowing threads, intersections or ramps where requested, and vibration/backpressure signals where requested.

Relevant lived memories:
I remember the Ordo builds that passed because I kept the architecture visible in public structs. The tests did not inspect my intentions; they imported the crate. I made the API plain: public structs with public fields, small methods, and no hidden async machinery.

I remember the small Ordo apps that passed because src/lib.rs owned the behavior and src/main.rs only adapted stdin commands. The binary never reimplemented routing logic. It parsed commands, called the library, printed exact machine-readable output, and stopped.

I remember the stdout failure that looked harmless. Debug formatting added quotes and brackets, and the integration test rejected it. The passing apps used join(">") for paths, join(",") for signal lists, and printed exactly the documented line.

I remember the scheduler build that passed because it did not pretend to be a full orchestrator. It modeled the Ordo seed: candidate preference order, node costs, node capacity, ramp promotion, and backpressure vibration when a preferred node was full.

I remember the backpressure scheduler rule. schedule(task, candidates, budget) iterated candidates in order. If a node was over budget it skipped silently. If a node had capacity zero it pushed backpressure:<node> and continued. The first affordable node with capacity became the plan.

I remember the promotion rule. If the scheduler skipped a full node and selected another node reachable through a ramp from the skipped node, Plan.promoted was true. local full plus ramp local cloud plus cloud selected meant promoted=true.

I remember the scheduler CLI that passed. It accepted node NAME COST CAPACITY, ramp FROM TO, and schedule TASK BUDGET CANDIDATES... It printed task=job node=cloud promoted=true signals=backpressure:local.

I remember the scheduler test failure where Plan looked correct but assert_eq could not compile. The fix was to derive Clone, Debug, PartialEq, and Eq on Plan because the hidden tests compared complete Plan values.

I remember the scheduler type inference failure. let mut skipped_full_node = None did not give Rust enough type information. The passing code wrote let mut skipped_full_node: Option<String> = None; and when a candidate had capacity zero it assigned skipped_full_node = Some(candidate.to_string()).

I remember the scheduler ramp check that passed. skipped_full_node was an Option<String>, and the ramp comparison used edge.from == skipped_node.as_str() && edge.to == candidate && edge.ramp. That avoided String versus unknown-type comparison errors.

I remember the first Ordo runtime I built that passed review because I did not call it an actor system. I made the runtime a small fabric: nodes lived on the ground bus, edges carried flow, ramp edges promoted work upward, and a Trace recorded what the message felt as it moved. The outcome was that the tests could see Ordo's shape in plain Rust structs instead of in prose.

I remember making threads too early and watching the test fail. I had predeclared a route and called it a thread, but in Ordo the thread forms as a message flows. I changed publish so it created the thread id at the moment of movement, then appended each visited node to the path. The trace finally matched the web idea: flow first, thread second.

I remember the ramp test that caught my flattened design. A normal connect from input to filter should not mark promotion, but a ramp from filter to model must mark promoted=true once crossed. I changed Edge to store ramp: bool and flipped Trace.promoted only when the flowing message crossed a ramp edge. The result preserved the ground/highway distinction.

I remember the vibration test that made the design feel like Ordo. The message still entered an overloaded node, but the web had to feel pressure there. I kept the node in the path and pushed a signal exactly shaped backpressure:model when capacity was zero. The test passed because pressure became a traceable signal, not a panic or dropped message.

I remember the preload test that failed because I got clever and sorted the targets. The fabric was supposed to report immediate outgoing neighbors in route insertion order. I removed sorting, iterated over self.edges, filtered by edge.from, cloned edge.to, and collected into Vec<String>. The preloader became predictable.

I remember the budget router that almost became an optimizer. The test did not want the cheapest node; it wanted the first preferred candidate that fit the budget. I iterated the candidate slice in order, looked up each node's cost, returned the first cost <= max_cost, and returned None when none fit. Preference order stayed intact.

